WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.649
This lesson walks through building the fix into the tool layer, where an ambiguous empty result can

2
00:00:05.649 --> 00:00:08.102
be caught before it ever reaches the agent.

3
00:00:08.600 --> 00:00:14.126
We are going to walk through the wrapper patterns, the status schema, the retry rules, and the

4
00:00:14.126 --> 00:00:17.301
logging practices that together make ambiguous nothing

5
00:00:17.879 --> 00:00:19.319
impossible to return.

6
00:00:19.319 --> 00:00:21.200
Let's get into it.

7
00:00:21.200 --> 00:00:26.760
The fix for this bug is structural, and it lives in the plumbing between the tool and the agent.

8
00:00:26.959 --> 00:00:32.680
A tool wrapper is a thin layer sitting there. It does one job. It normalizes the response,

9
00:00:33.200 --> 00:00:36.000
attaches a status field, and enforces disambiguation

10
00:00:36.880 --> 00:00:42.883
before the agent sees anything. A status enum is the structured field the wrapper attaches. It

11
00:00:42.883 --> 00:00:44.416
labels the outcome class

12
00:00:44.799 --> 00:00:50.919
explicitly, so the agent branches on that label rather than inferring meaning from an empty data payload.

13
00:00:51.439 --> 00:00:54.639
And retry discipline is the rule that governs what happens next.

14
00:00:55.200 --> 00:00:57.439
Empty results don't trigger retries.

15
00:00:57.959 --> 00:01:04.480
Access failures do, up to a configured limit. That rule has to live in the wrapper, not the agent prompt, or

16
00:01:04.839 --> 00:01:06.639
it will be applied

17
00:01:06.639 --> 00:01:08.639
inconsistently across sessions and tool calls.

18
00:01:09.639 --> 00:01:16.680
The first wrapper rule is distinguishing HTTP success with an empty body from HTTP failure.

19
00:01:16.919 --> 00:01:18.959
These are fundamentally different outcomes,

20
00:01:18.959 --> 00:01:22.120
and they need to be treated differently at the wrapper layer. A

21
00:01:22.680 --> 00:01:29.720
200 response with an empty list is a valid empty result. The server processed your query, checked the index, and

22
00:01:29.959 --> 00:01:37.040
came back with nothing. That is meaningful information. A 4x or 5x response is an access failure,

23
00:01:37.440 --> 00:01:43.244
regardless of what the body says. The data source rejected or couldn't process the request, and a

24
00:01:43.244 --> 00:01:44.980
timeout is an access failure,

25
00:01:45.480 --> 00:01:48.959
even when no HTTP status code is returned at all.

26
00:01:49.319 --> 00:01:54.519
The wrapper catches all three of these cases and maps each one to the correct status field

27
00:01:54.720 --> 00:01:59.519
before the agent ever sees the response. That mapping is the core of the wrapper contract.

28
00:02:00.360 --> 00:02:04.839
The status enum has four outcome classes, and each one has to be named

29
00:02:05.480 --> 00:02:07.480
explicitly in the agent prompt.

30
00:02:07.519 --> 00:02:13.559
Success means the tool reached the data source and returned matching results, so the agent proceeds normally.

31
00:02:13.919 --> 00:02:19.792
Empty result means the tool reached the source and confirmed no matching content exists, so the

32
00:02:19.792 --> 00:02:21.338
absence is a real answer.

33
00:02:22.039 --> 00:02:27.440
Partial result means the response was truncated, so the agent flags that the answer may be incomplete.

34
00:02:28.080 --> 00:02:32.960
Access failure means the tool never reached the source, so the agent draws no conclusions,

35
00:02:33.199 --> 00:02:37.039
applies the retry policy, and tells the user it couldn't check.

36
00:02:37.720 --> 00:02:41.199
Implicit defaults are where the bug re-enters. Leave any one of those branches

37
00:02:41.279 --> 00:02:44.479
unnamed, and the model fills the gap with its own assumption.

38
00:02:45.639 --> 00:02:50.520
Retry policy is one of the clearest expressions of the disambiguation principle in practice.

39
00:02:51.000 --> 00:02:56.465
Empty results don't retry. If the data source confirmed there is no matching content, running the

40
00:02:56.465 --> 00:02:58.663
same query again against the same index

41
00:02:59.160 --> 00:03:01.160
produces the same empty result.

42
00:03:01.600 --> 00:03:06.320
Retrying wastes latency and burns through quota with zero benefit to the user.

43
00:03:07.080 --> 00:03:12.479
Access failures are different. The data source was unreachable, and that condition may be transient.

44
00:03:13.000 --> 00:03:19.559
A network blip, a brief service restart, or a temporary credential expiration can all resolve on their own.

45
00:03:20.199 --> 00:03:24.440
Retrying with exponential backoff is appropriate, up to a configured limit.

46
00:03:24.720 --> 00:03:32.199
Once that limit is hit, the wrapper should escalate, return an access failure status, log the exhausted retries, and

47
00:03:32.479 --> 00:03:36.839
surface the failure to the agent, rather than silently returning empty to the caller.

48
00:03:38.000 --> 00:03:43.240
Every access failure needs a structured log entry at the moment it occurs, not

49
00:03:43.520 --> 00:03:48.479
reconstructed later from the agent's output. The log entry should capture four things.

50
00:03:48.679 --> 00:03:55.479
The timestamp of the failed call, the tool name and target endpoint, the error class, timeout, auth,

51
00:03:56.039 --> 00:04:00.759
connection, or partial, and the retry strategy that was applied along with its outcome.

52
00:04:01.199 --> 00:04:08.119
Without this log, post-incident diagnosis becomes guesswork. From the outside, the agent session looks clean.

53
00:04:08.399 --> 00:04:12.320
The tool ran, the agent responded. No exception was raised.

54
00:04:12.520 --> 00:04:19.079
The failure is invisible unless you built the trace in from the start. When you're debugging a bad decision that

55
00:04:19.320 --> 00:04:24.818
propagated through an agent session three days ago, a structured log is the difference between a

56
00:04:24.818 --> 00:04:26.822
quick investigation and a multi-day

57
00:04:27.320 --> 00:04:29.000
reconstruction effort.

58
00:04:29.000 --> 00:04:34.839
User-facing language has to track the status field exactly. Three phrases, three distinct uses.

59
00:04:35.239 --> 00:04:39.079
Found nothing is reserved for confirmed empty results only.

60
00:04:39.559 --> 00:04:44.239
When you say that, you are asserting that the search succeeded and no data exists.

61
00:04:44.399 --> 00:04:47.880
That phrase should never appear when an access failure occurred.

62
00:04:48.320 --> 00:04:51.160
Couldn't check is the correct phrase for access failures.

63
00:04:51.160 --> 00:04:57.228
It signals that the absence of results isn't confirmed, and the user shouldn't act on it as if it

64
00:04:57.228 --> 00:04:58.417
were a real answer.

65
00:04:59.000 --> 00:05:03.119
And partial results is what you say when the tool flagged a partial response.

66
00:05:03.359 --> 00:05:08.799
It tells the user that some data may be missing, and the answer they are getting isn't complete.

67
00:05:09.160 --> 00:05:14.809
These three phrases are the user-facing expression of the same disambiguation the wrapper enforces

68
00:05:14.809 --> 00:05:16.942
at the technical layer of the system.

69
00:05:17.959 --> 00:05:23.440
The status field in the tool response schema has to be mandatory, not optional.

70
00:05:24.119 --> 00:05:31.320
Optional status fields lead to exactly the failure mode you're trying to prevent. Some tool calls return a status and

71
00:05:31.720 --> 00:05:37.959
disambiguate correctly, and others return only data and silently revert to the ambiguous nothing problem.

72
00:05:37.959 --> 00:05:43.678
If the field is optional, some developer will leave it out under time pressure, and the wrapper will

73
00:05:43.678 --> 00:05:46.022
have no status to attach to the response.

74
00:05:46.359 --> 00:05:50.839
The status field should also be a typed enum, not a free text string.

75
00:05:51.440 --> 00:05:56.480
Strings allow access failure, access failure, failed, error, and

76
00:05:57.000 --> 00:06:02.679
timeout to all appear as distinct values, which breaks the agent's branching logic.

77
00:06:02.959 --> 00:06:06.279
A typed enum enforces exactly four valid states.

78
00:06:06.559 --> 00:06:13.440
The wrapper populates this field before the agent ever sees the response, and that is the contract you're enforcing.

79
00:06:14.880 --> 00:06:18.600
Disambiguation is an architectural commitment built into every layer.

80
00:06:19.040 --> 00:06:25.839
The wrapper maps outcomes to a status, the schema makes the status mandatory, the agent prompt

81
00:06:26.119 --> 00:06:31.079
branches on it, the retry policy respects it, and the log records it.

82
00:06:31.519 --> 00:06:37.323
When each of those layers enforces the same distinction between access failure and empty result, the

83
00:06:37.323 --> 00:06:38.541
silent false negative

84
00:06:38.799 --> 00:06:40.799
has nowhere left to hide.

85
00:06:40.959 --> 00:06:43.760
That wraps up this lesson. I'll see you in the next one.

